  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at half its value as a surface with 8 contours. Steps start overshooting along x above learning rate 1/2.4 ≈ 0.42 and grow (diverge) above 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83.",
    "Each step is -rate × (slope + noise). Noise per component has spread 1.2 × sqrt((64 − batch) / (63 × batch)): largest at batch 1 and exactly zero at 64. With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps (rate × slope estimate) drawn at 2.5 times their true length, one scale for both. Mini-batch step: the move the ball makes. Full-data step: against the true slope.",
    "A run stops after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 6 (diverged). A diverged ball and path are drawn at the bowl's rim; the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The ball takes one step every 4 host ticks (7.5 steps a second at speed 1). Take one step pauses the run and moves it exactly one step.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence. Each new run uses the next noise values. The trail and the ghost hold at most 40 steps each, plus the start."
  ]
}